\section{Reduced-rank spatial factors}{

A single areal field term (\code{icar(graph = adj)}, \code{car_proper(graph = adj)}, or
\code{bym2(graph = adj)}) on the occupancy \code{formula} fits the reduced-rank (HMSC /
spatial-gllvm) community model: \code{K} shared latent fields with per-species
loadings on the occupancy predictor,
\verb{logit psi_sc = X_c (mu_occ + b_occ_s) + sum_k L_sk w_kc}. A single shared
field with only a species intercept can shift each map's level but not its
shape; the loadings give each species its own spatial shape as a combination
of the shared factors, borrowing strength so rare taxa get a calibrated map.
The number of factors is set by \code{control = list(n.factors = K)}, or chosen
automatically by \code{control = list(n.factors = "auto", n.factors.max = M)},
which fits the identified (lower-triangular loading) ladder and picks the rank
that maximises the empirical-Bayes Laplace marginal likelihood (the field is
integrated out, so its prior supplies the Occam penalty -- latent-level
criteria such as WAIC track the field's effective dimension, not the rank, and
under-select). The per-rank evidence is returned in \code{fit$spatial$K_selection}.
\code{sd.load} sets the loading prior scale.

Adding the SAME \code{icar(graph = adj)} term to the cover \code{positive} formula
shares the latent fields across the two processes: the fields then also load on
the cover predictor through a free loading matrix,
\verb{g(cover)_sc = X_c (mu_pos + b_pos_s) + sum_k Lpos_sk w_kc}, so a species'
spatial occupancy pattern and its spatial cover pattern are linked through one
set of factors. The cover loadings are returned in \code{fit$spatial$loadings_cover}.
The field is shared, so the cover-arm term must match the occupancy arm (same
type and graph), and a cover-arm field without a matching occupancy field errors.

The field structure is set by the term: \code{icar()} (improper intrinsic CAR, the
default), \code{car_proper(graph = adj)} (a proper CAR whose per-factor correlation
\code{rho} is returned in \code{fit$spatial$rho_w}), or \code{bym2(graph = adj)} (the
Riebler 2016 convolution whose per-factor spatial-variance fraction \code{phi} is
returned in \code{fit$spatial$phi_w}); the field hyperparameter is estimated by EM.
All three share one engine; \code{fit$spatial$field_type} records the choice.
Structured terms on the detection arm, or an unsupported field term, error from
the dispatcher.

The shared fields imply a residual species-association matrix (the
spatial-JSDM / HMSC output): \code{\link[=tobs_associations]{tobs_associations()}} returns the occupancy
association \verb{corr(L L')} and, with a cover-arm factor, the cover association
and the cross-arm occupancy-vs-cover association, each marginalised over the
loading posterior so an interval accompanies the estimate. \code{predict()} returns
the other JSDM output -- the per-species per-cell occupancy maps (\code{psi} with
\code{psi_lower} / \code{psi_upper}), marginalised over the loading + field posterior so
a rare species borrows strength across the shared factors for a calibrated map.
